The character moved to (-7.1,-6.4) despite idle controls and footYaw auto-align. Wait, after `playerInput(null)`, the real-time loop ran for 0.3s? It moved from (-18.4,1.86) to (-7.1,-6.4) — 13.7 units in 0.65s (0.4 sim + real-time). It was sprinting at 17.2 and decelerates with damp 13: distance in 0.4s ~ 17.2/13*(1-e^-5.2)=1.31. But it moved 13.7. So the character kept sprinting for over 0.6s. That means `playerInput(null)` did not clear the input? Look at InputState.sample:

```
if (this.debugControls) { return {...}; }
```
If debugControls is null, it proceeds to real controls: no keys, touch inactive, no gamepad. It should return throttle 0. After simulate(0.4), distance ~1.3, not 13.7.

But the `simulate(0.4)` call happened; then Playwright `press F` (takes some time), then sleep .3, then eval. Real-time between could be several seconds of wall clock with the game loop running at ~10fps -> simTime advances. If 0.4s idle sim + maybe 1.5s real sim, distance ~2.2. Still not 13.7.

Hmm, maybe `playerInput(null)` sets `input.debugControls = null` but the character movement is also influenced by camera auto-align; no.

Wait the score is now 0-1 (banner GOAL). A bot goal happened, which calls resetKickoff -> enterCar(true) should have set onFoot=false. But onFoot is true! And the character at (-7.1,-6.4) is far. Something's off: after the goal, resetKickoff should enter the car. But `mode` might be 'goal' still at the time (goalTimer 2.35). The banner says GOAL, so we're in goal mode; onFoot remains true until resetKickoff. During goal mode, updateGoalState calls `character.update(dt, idle, footYaw, effects)` with idle; speed was high, then decelerates. But the character moved 13.7 during goal mode? It had been at (-18.4,1.86) sprinting when the goal happened, then goal mode for up to 2.35s with idle controls; it would coast with damp 13 to a stop over ~1s, distance ~1.3. So still not 13.7.

Actually wait, the previous eval that produced foot3 ended with speed 17.2 at (-18.4,1.86). Then the next eval `playerInput(null); simulate(0.4)` — but there may have been a long gap between evals (screenshot command took time). During that gap, the real-time loop kept running with debugControls still set (sprint), so the character kept moving for seconds before we cleared it. That explains the extra distance. Good — my mistake. Need to clear debug input immediately after each test or use a single eval for the whole scenario.

Also the bot scored during that gap, and mode is goal at the start of the next eval; `simulate()` returns `{error:'cannot simulate in mode goal'}`? It allows mode goal, so it ran updateGoalState. The character idled.

F key press during goal mode: `if (mode !== 'playing') return` in exit/enter? enterCar doesn't check mode; it would enter the car during goal mode if near. But it was far so showed TOO FAR? The banner shows GOAL because showBanner for goal; enterCar far called showBanner('TOO FAR') maybe then goal banner overwritten? Actually the goal banner set at goal time; then F far would set TOO FAR. But we see GOAL, so perhaps the F press didn't reach because the debug input is null now and the keydown in goal mode still sets interactQueued; updatePlaying isn't running, so interact isn't processed. Right: input.sample only in updatePlaying. So F queued and will be processed when play resumes. Then resetKickoff enters car silently and clears onFoot anyway. Fine.

Let's run a single, clean eval for the interaction test:
- if mode !== playing, wait? We can call g.start() to reset, then set ball far x=40, exit car.
- Walk away from the car a bit with debug input 0.5s, clear.
- Press F via a DOM KeyboardEvent? To test the actual listener, dispatch `window.dispatchEvent(new KeyboardEvent('keydown',{code:'KeyF'}))` inside eval. Then step 1/60 to process. Check onFoot and banner.
- Walk back with debug input, clear, dispatch F, step, check onFoot false.

But dispatching a synthetic keyboard event works with the input listener. Yes.

Also verify touch use button click: `document.getElementById('useBtn').dispatchEvent(new PointerEvent('pointerdown',{pointerId:1,bubbles:true}))`. bindHold uses pointerdown. Then pointerup. Could test later on mobile.

Let's do the whole scenario in one eval to avoid real-time drift:

```js
g.start(); g.setBall(40,2.05,0,0,0,0);
g.exitCar();
// walk away
g.playerInput({throttle:1,steer:0,boost:true});
g.simulate(0.9,1/60);
g.playerInput(null);
const distAfterWalk = ...
// dispatch F far
window.dispatchEvent(new KeyboardEvent('keydown',{code:'KeyF'}));
g.step(1/60);
const far = {onFoot, bannerText, ...}
// walk back toward car
g.playerInput({throttle:-1,steer:0,boost:true});
g.simulate(0.9,1/60);
g.playerInput(null);
const distBack ...
// dispatch F
window.dispatchEvent(new KeyboardEvent('keydown',{code:'KeyF'}));
g.step(1/60);
const near = ...
```

But note: while walking away for 0.9s at sprint, the character goes from -30,3.6 toward footYaw=0 => +X, to about -15. Then F far -> enterCar checks distance > 8 -> TOO FAR. Good. Then walk back with throttle -1 for 0.9s -> moves back near the car (maybe -28). Then F -> enter. The bot may score meanwhile, but 1.8s sim; ball at x=40, bot at +30 chasing ball, unlikely to score quickly. Even if a goal occurs, the test might reset. We'll see.

Let's run.

